  "nameStory": "The 'dwarf' in this plant's name comes from its petite stature compared to its towering cousin, Great St. John's Wort, which can reach six feet tall. Like other St. John's worts, it's named after Saint John the Baptist because many species in this family bloom around his feast day (June 24th). This little member of the family keeps the tradition but does it closer to the ground!",
  "description": "Dwarf St. John's wort is a small, delicate wildflower that rarely grows taller than your knee. This branching annual or short-lived perennial creates a bushy, upright form with multiple stems spreading outward. Its tiny yellow flowers cluster at the stem tips, creating cheerful splashes of color in wet areas. Despite its small size, it can form impressive colonies in the right conditions.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Flowers",
      "description": "Tiny yellow flowers less than a quarter-inch across with five petals, clustered in branching groups at stem tips. The flowers feel papery and delicate to the touch.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Leaves",
      "description": "Small oval leaves about half an inch to an inch long, arranged opposite each other on the stem. Hold them up to the light and you'll see tiny translucent dots - these are oil glands.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth Habit",
      "description": "Multi-branched and bushy, typically 6-24 inches tall with an upright but spreading form. Stems feel smooth and somewhat square in cross-section.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Fruit/Seeds",
      "description": "Small reddish-brown capsules that split open when ripe, releasing tiny seeds. The capsules persist after flowers fade, giving a rusty appearance.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for dwarf St. John's wort in wet, sandy soils along pond edges, stream banks, and marshy areas throughout New England. It thrives in disturbed wetland areas and can often be found along muddy trail edges or in seasonal pools. This moisture-loving plant prefers partial sun to light shade.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Young plants emerge as small rosettes of oval leaves. Early growth is often hard to spot among other emerging wetland vegetation.",
    "summer": "Peak blooming season from July through early September, when tiny yellow flowers are most visible and identification is easiest.",
    "fall": "Flowers fade and small seed capsules turn reddish-brown. Plants may turn yellowish before dying back if they're annuals.",
    "winter": "Annual plants die completely, while perennial forms die back to the roots. Only dried stems and seed capsules may remain visible."
  },
  "funFact": "Those tiny dots you can see in the leaves when held to light are actually miniature oil glands that contain the same compounds found in expensive mood-support supplements - this little wetland plant is essentially nature's pharmacy growing in a puddle!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most easily confused with other small St. John's worts, but dwarf St. John's wort prefers much wetter conditions than most of its relatives. Remember: if it's growing with its feet wet in marshy soil, you're likely looking at the dwarf species rather than the common field varieties.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Don't confuse any St. John's worts with actual worts (unrelated plants) or assume they're all the same medicinally - while Common St. John's Wort is widely used herbally, dwarf St. John's wort is much less studied and concentrated. The family name comes from an ancient Greek tradition, not from any particular healing properties.",
  "compareHints": "Among New England's St. John's worts, dwarf St. John's wort is distinguished by its preference for wet habitats and its particularly small flowers and leaves. While Common St. John's Wort grows in dry fields with larger flowers, and pineweed has needle-like leaves, dwarf St. John's wort sticks to marshy areas with its tiny oval leaves and delicate flower clusters. Its wetland habitat is often the biggest clue to its identity.",
